## Is acne a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. A long-term skin condition that affects everyday activities, such as using your hands, sleeping or getting dressed, can be covered. The law also treats a **severe disfigurement** as having a substantial effect, even if it does not affect what you can do. This may apply to severe acne scarring.

## At work

Most people with acne do not need changes at work. It may help to have time off for dermatology appointments. If uniform or equipment, such as masks or helmets, make acne worse, talk to your employer.

## Benefits

Acne does not qualify for disability benefits such as PIP. If acne is affecting your mental health, read about [depression](/conditions/mental-health/depression/).

## Costs

Under-16s, and 16 to 18 year olds in full-time education, get **free prescriptions** in England. A **prescription prepayment certificate** can save money if you need several items. Prescriptions are free in Wales. Try our [NHS health costs check](/support/health-costs/checker/).
